  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 416 AM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nAn abnormally strong upper-level ridge is centered over the Four\nCorners region today, and looks to remain there through late next\nweek. To the east, the Bermuda High remains in place over the\nwestern Atlantic. But that will actually retrograde toward the\nsoutheastern CONUS and Gulf Coast by early next week. Meanwhile,\nwesterly flow with the jet stream will remain deflected across the\nnorthern CONUS until later next week when a more pronounced\nridge-trough pattern establishes over the country.\n\nOur forecast area and broader region will therefore remain mostly\nbeneath the weak troughing pattern between the ridges into next \nweek. PWAT values remain soupy with onshore flow and deep tropical\nmoisture being reinforced with an easterly wave Saturday into\nSunday. Given the lack of strong ridging overhead, reinforcing\nmoisture influx, and daily chances for showers and storms, the\nconfidence in widespread 105-110 heat index values is not great\nenough to justify Heat Advisory headlines at this point in time.\nPeak heat index values each day will be near 105 degrees,\nespecially over the next several days. \"Limited\" heat stress\nmessaging is in line with the expected forecast, but this kind of\nhot and humid weather is still dangerous for sensitive\npopulations, especially the elderly or people without functioning\nair conditioning in the home. Care should be taken to limit\nstrenuous activities in mid day, and frequent breaks with cool\nwater and shade are advised for anyone working or playing outdoors\nthis time of year. /NF/",
